WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Photograph a nearby star tonight.

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.760
Then photograph it again six months later.

3
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.660
Against the far more
distant stars behind it,

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.060
it seems to have moved, by a tiny angle.

5
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.220
Nothing about the star changed.

6
00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.130
Earth carried us to the
other side of its orbit.

7
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.800
How can that tiny yearly shift
tell us how far away the star is,

8
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.780
and then how big it is?

9
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:28.510
In this mission, you'll meet a unit of
distance built from that very angle.

10
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.920
Then you'll read a star's temperature
from the peak of its light,

11
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.110
and combine it with its
power to find its size.

12
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.340
Your mission: explain how a tiny
yearly shift reveals a star's distance,

13
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.800
and then its size.
